 ## Overview
-Resolution Adaptation modulates spatial sampling density or grid resolution during volume rendering to balance visual fidelity against computational cost [G]. It solves fixed-performance bottlenecks by dynamically adjusting the rendering budget based on metrics like frame time, training iteration, or spatial complexity [G]. Unlike static resolution methods, this family continuously or periodically adapts sampling rates, distinguishing it from siblings like [Random Pixel Subsampling](1668-random-pixel-subsampling.md) (alters ray count) and [Two-stage NeRF Rendering](925-two-stage-nerf-rendering.md) (coarse-to-fine refinement) [G].
+Resolution Adaptation refers to a family of techniques that modulate the spatial sampling density or grid resolution during volume rendering to balance visual fidelity against computational cost [G]. It solves the problem of fixed-performance bottlenecks by dynamically adjusting the rendering budget based on real-time metrics such as frame time, training iteration, or spatial complexity [G]. Unlike static resolution methods, this family adapts the "anchor points" or sampling rates continuously or periodically, distinguishing it from siblings like [Random Pixel Subsampling](1668-random-pixel-subsampling.md) which alters ray count rather than spatial resolution, and [Two-stage NeRF Rendering](925-two-stage-nerf-rendering.md) which uses coarse-to-fine refinement rather than global or local resolution scaling [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-Builds on the parent's shared principles [Adaptive Sampling and Resolution](1412-adaptive-sampling-and-resolution.md).
+The core mechanism shared by all children is the deviation from a fixed, uniform sampling strategy [G]. Instead of casting a constant number of rays or samples per pixel/ray, the method computes a metric (such as gradient magnitude, rendering error, or temporal difference) and uses it to modulate the sampling rate or resolution [G].
+
+Mathematically, this often involves a control loop:
+1.  **Metric Evaluation:** Compute a scalar value $M$ representing local complexity or error [G].
+2.  **Adaptation:** Adjust a parameter $P$ (step size $\Delta t$, resolution $R$, or sampling probability $w$) based on $M$ [G].
+3.  **Rendering/Training:** Execute the volume integral or loss computation with the adjusted parameter [G].
+
+Common adaptation laws include:
+*   **Step Size Adaptation:** $\Delta t \propto \frac{1}{M}$ or $\Delta t = \min(\Delta t_{max}, \max(\Delta t_{min}, \frac{k}{M}))$ [G].
+*   **Resolution Control:** $R_{new} = R_{old} + \alpha \cdot (t_{target} - t_{actual})$ or threshold-based switching [G].
+*   **Probability Weighting:** $w_i \propto M_i$ for importance sampling [G].
+
+All methods in this family aim to maintain visual fidelity while reducing the total number of volume queries [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md)
-Maintains interactive frame rates by dynamically changing rendering resolution to match computational budget. Sets itself apart by adapting sampling density or geometric approximation in real-time based on performance metrics, rather than using static pre-computed hierarchies. Choose when interactive manipulation requires trading visual fidelity for speed.
+Level-of-detail (LOD) methods maintain interactive frame rates during viewpoint interaction by dynamically changing the resolution of the rendering to match the available computational budget. Unlike static pre-computed LOD hierarchies, these methods adapt the sampling density or geometric approximation in real-time based on rendering performance metrics. This approach solves the problem of high computational cost in volume rendering by trading visual fidelity for speed during interactive manipulation, switching to higher quality only when the view is static or when the frame budget allows.
 
 ### [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md)
-Dynamically adjusts voxel grid resolution and active spatial bounding box during neural volume training. Sets itself apart by coupling resolution upscaling with alpha mask updates to prune empty background space. Choose when training efficiency and memory usage are bottlenecks.
+This method dynamically adjusts the voxel grid resolution and the active spatial bounding box (AABB) of a neural volume representation during training. It solves the problem of inefficient memory usage and slow convergence by initially training in a low-resolution, coarse space and progressively refining the grid density and spatial extent as the model learns [G]. It differs from static resolution methods by coupling resolution upscaling with alpha mask updates, ensuring that the volume only occupies the space containing visible geometry, thereby pruning empty background space.
 
 ### [Dynamic Resolution Adaptation](2683-dynamic-resolution-adaptation.md)
-Runtime technique adjusting render viewport reduction factor based on previous frame time and allocated budget to maintain target frame rate. Sets itself apart by continuously shifting resolution levels based on frame time gradients rather than fixed blending factors. Choose when parametric changes cause performance instability or cybersickness.
+Dynamic Resolution Adaptation is a runtime technique that adjusts the render viewport reduction factor based on previous frame render time and an allocated time budget to maintain a target frame rate. It solves the problem of performance instability in volume rendering, where small parametric changes (e.g., transfer function modifications) can cause frame rate dips leading to cybersickness. It sets itself apart from static resolution schemes by continuously shifting the "anchor points" between resolution levels based on the gradient of recent frame times, rather than using fixed blending factors.
 
 ### [Mixed-Resolution Multi-Volume Rendering](3193-mixed-resolution-multi-volume-rendering.md)
-Renders different channels of a multi-volume dataset at different resolution levels to save memory and reduce samples. Sets itself apart by operating on a per-channel basis within a single ray traversal, rather than switching dataset resolution globally. Choose when channels have varying frequency content or visual importance.
+Mixed-resolution multi-volume rendering is a technique where different channels of a multi-volume dataset are rendered at different resolution levels to save memory and reduce sample evaluation. It addresses the inefficiency of applying a uniform high resolution to all channels when some possess lower frequency content or lower visual importance. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) by operating on a per-channel basis within a single ray traversal, rather than switching the entire dataset's resolution or using spatial partitioning for the whole volume [G].
 
 ### [Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering](4614-volume-level-of-detail-lod-rendering.md)
-Maximizes image quality within frame rate and hardware constraints by adaptively modifying rendered octree cells. Sets itself apart by maintaining an active cut of the octree that splits/merges nodes every frame, explicitly managing hierarchical non-leaf nodes rather than just alpha masks or global scales. Choose when maintaining interactivity for large hierarchical datasets.
+Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering is a method that maximizes image quality within user-specified frame rate and system constraints (RAM, texture memory, bandwidth) by adaptively modifying the set of rendered octree cells. It solves the problem of maintaining interactivity for large datasets by dynamically adjusting the resolution of the volume representation based on viewing parameters and hardware limits. Unlike static LOD methods that precompute fixed levels, this approach maintains an "active cut" of the octree, splitting or merging nodes every frame to balance visual fidelity against rendering cost. It differs from sibling methods like [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) by explicitly managing a hierarchical cut of non-leaf nodes rather than just updating alpha masks or global resolution scales.
 
 ### [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md)
-Balances processing between a high-detail focus region and a lower-detail context region to improve interactivity. Sets itself apart by decomposing rendering into two passes with distinct parameters, explicitly trading seamless integration for speed. Choose when hardware resources are limited and time-critical applications require prioritized detail.
+Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering balances processing capacity between a high-detail focus region and a lower-detail context region to improve interactivity in time-critical applications. It solves the problem of limited hardware resources by decomposing the rendering task into two passes with distinct parameters, explicitly trading seamless integration for speed.
 
 ### [Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB)](8219-multiresolution-atlas-buffer-mab.md)
-Acceleration wrapper for DVR in VR that reduces ray density based on peripheral vision and HMD lens distortion, reconstructing full resolution via compositing. Sets itself apart by operating as a bracket around existing raycasters (modifying ray construction and compositing) rather than modifying traversal or volume resolution, and not requiring eye-tracking hardware. Choose for VR applications needing high refresh rates without eye-tracking.
+The Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB) is an acceleration wrapper for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) in Virtual Reality (VR) that reduces ray density adaptively based on peripheral vision and Head-Mounted Display (HMD) lens distortion, then reconstructs the full-resolution image via compositing. It differs from standard foveated rendering by not requiring high-speed eye-tracking hardware; instead, it leverages the fixed nature of HMD lenses and the non-uniform resolution of the human visual system to create a static, gaze-centered resolution map. Unlike sibling methods that modify the ray traversal itself (e.g., [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md)) or dynamic volume resolution ([Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md)), MAB operates as a "bracket" around existing raycasters, modifying ray construction and post-traversal compositing.
 
 #### Mixed Resolution Scene Rendering
-Combines full-resolution scene elements with low-resolution high-density volume data by rendering the volume to off-screen targets and projecting them onto a quad [S1]. Setup involves two off-screen cameras (one per eye) updating each frame, two low-resolution render targets (e.g., 200x200), and a quad moving in front of the user [S1]. Each frame: draw low-resolution render targets for each eye (volume data, expensive shaders), draw the scene normally at full resolution (meshes, UI), and draw the quad projecting the low-res renders onto it [S1].
